    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d88">

                <pb facs="0317" n="277" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg277"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">780.5/9–2252</note>
                <head>No. 88<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of
                            State to the Secretary of Defense (Lovett)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-09-22">September 22, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: Attached is a
                        memorandum<anchor n="1" xml:id="d88-f1"/> setting forth proposed United
                    States comments on certain proposals concerning the Middle East Defense
                    Organization put forward by the French Government in a memorandum handed to the
                    Department of State August 15, 1952 (copy attached).<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d88-f2"/> A copy of this memorandum was transmitted informally to
                    the Department of Defense at the time of its receipt.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d88-f3"/></p>
                <p>The French memorandum is addressed principally to the question of the higher
                    military direction of MEDO and emphasizes the
                    importance the French attach to making the NATO
                    Standing Group responsible for the guidance and direction of MEDO. In view of the interest of the Department of
                    Defense in this matter, I would appreciate your advising me as to whether you
                    consider the proposed <pb facs="0318" n="278" xml:id="pg278"/> memorandum to the
                    French an appropriate expression of United States views.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#small-caps">Henry A. Byroade</hi><lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Assistant Secretary</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
